\section{Fixed and refitted parameters}{
Fixed parameters, the default, suit a check of the calibration of a model.
The reference of a correct model (see "How to read the area") repeats the
same procedure, with the parameters estimated from all the observations of
each simulated dataset, so the area of the data is judged against areas
found in the same way. They are also much faster.

To compare models by their scores, use \code{refit = TRUE}. With fixed
parameters the test observations have helped to estimate each model, so
its scores are optimistic, and more so for a model with more parameters.
The comparison then favours the model with more parameters, and more so
when the test sets are spatial clusters. A refit costs one fit for each
test set and model, and it can fail when a training set is too small for
a model, which leaves the scores of that test set missing (see
"Failures").
}

\section{Predictive distributions}{
The observations of a test set are predicted jointly, with the nugget and
the random effects when the model has them. A random effect has its
distribution given the training data, and a level that the training data
don't have is drawn from the distribution of the random effect. The scores
of an observation are those of its own predictive distribution. Test
observations with missing values in a variable used by any of the models
are left out.

The memory of a test set grows with the square of its number of locations
and the time of its joint draws with the cube, so large datasets are better
divided into more test sets.

For a Gaussian model, the predictive distribution is Gaussian, with the
mean and the standard deviation of the draws, which include the
measurement error. The predictive distribution of a binomial, Poisson or
negative binomial count \eqn{Y} is the distribution of the family given
the mean, averaged over the \eqn{J} draws of the mean from its own
predictive distribution,
\deqn{F(k) = \frac{1}{J} \sum_{j=1}^{J} P(Y \le k \mid \mu_j), \quad
  k = 0, 1, 2, \ldots}
Here \eqn{\mu_j} is the \eqn{j}th draw of the prevalence or rate, and
\eqn{P(Y \le k \mid \mu_j)} also depends on the denominator and, for a
negative binomial model, on its size.
}

\section{Scores}{
Every score is a loss, so lower values are better. The CRPS is in the
units of the response, which for binomial, Poisson and negative binomial
models are counts (for example numbers of positive individuals), not
prevalences or rates. The SCRPS is reported as
\deqn{\frac{1}{2}\left[1 + \frac{\mathrm{CRPS}(F, y)}{c} + \log(2c)\right],
  \quad c = \frac{1}{2} E|X - X'|,}
with \eqn{X} and \eqn{X'} independent draws from the predictive
distribution \eqn{F}. This is the negative of the scaled CRPS of Bolin and
Wallin (2023), whose scores are higher for better predictions, so that the
CRPS and the SCRPS share one orientation. The SCRPS can be negative.

For counts,
\deqn{\mathrm{CRPS}(F, y) = \sum_{k \ge 0} [F(k) - 1(k \ge y)]^2, \quad
  c = \sum_{k \ge 0} F(k) [1 - F(k)].}
The scores of counts have no random step beyond the draws of the mean, as
no counts are simulated for them. A model therefore gets the same scores
whatever its position in \code{models}. Where counts above 100 are predicted,
\eqn{F} is computed on a grid of counts and is linear in between. The grid
is fine enough for a relative error of about \eqn{10^{-4}} or less in the
CRPS and in \eqn{c}.
}

\section{Calibration}{
The calibration curve of \eqn{n} test observations is
\deqn{\frac{1}{n} \sum_{i=1}^{n} G_i(u), \quad 0 \le u \le 1.}
For a Gaussian outcome, \eqn{G_i(u)} is 1 if \eqn{F_i(y_i) \le u} and 0
otherwise, so the curve is the empirical distribution function of the PIT
values. For a count it is the average nonrandomised PIT (AnPIT), with
\eqn{G_i(u)} equal to 0 up to \eqn{F_i(y_i - 1)}, to 1 from
\eqn{F_i(y_i)}, and linear in between (Czado \emph{et al.} 2009). \code{PIT_area} is
the area between the curve and the identity line, from 0 for a perfectly
calibrated curve to at most 0.5.
}

\section{How to read the area}{
The area of a correct model is not zero, because its observations are finite
in number, because neighbouring test observations are above or below their
predictions together, and because the parameters are estimated from the
same data. Its distribution is found by simulation.
\enumerate{
\item 999 datasets are simulated from a Gaussian working model of the fit. It
has the linear predictor of the model, with its spatial correlation,
nugget and random effects, observed with the measurement error of a
Gaussian model. For counts each observation has instead a Gaussian error
whose variance makes it about as informative about the linear predictor
as the count is.
\item In each dataset the covariance parameters of the working model are
estimated again, from all its observations, or from each training set
when \code{refit = TRUE}, and the test observations are predicted from their
training sets, as for the data. This gives each test observation a PIT
value. For counts, it is turned into a count by the predictive
distribution of the data at that observation.
\item Each dataset gives a calibration curve and an area, computed as for the
data. \code{PIT_area_expected} is the mean of these areas, and
\code{PIT_area_limit} the value that 95\% of them are below. The band of
\code{\link[=plot_cross_validation]{plot_cross_validation()}} is the joint envelope of the curves, computed
as in \code{\link[=variogram]{variogram()}}, which the whole curve of a correct model is inside
with a probability of 95\%.
}

An area below \code{PIT_area_limit} is one that a correct model would give, and an
area above it points to miscalibration. Below the limit, a smaller area, or
one closer to \code{PIT_area_expected}, doesn't mean a better model. It mostly
reflects chance, the number of observations and the dependence between them,
so the areas of models shouldn't be ranked. The CRPS and the SCRPS are for
comparing models. The area is also not sensitive to every departure. Too many
observations in both tails change it little, and the curve and the histogram
of \code{\link[=plot_cross_validation]{plot_cross_validation()}} show them. Nor does it find a departure that
the estimated parameters make up for, such as a missing covariate or
nugget. The scores compare such models.

The simulated datasets go through the same cross-validation as the data, with
their parameters estimated again, so the area of a correct model is above
\code{PIT_area_limit} in about one dataset in twenty. When the test sets are a few
spatial clusters, it is above the limit, or its curve outside the band,
somewhat more often. The time of the simulation grows about with the cube of
the number of observations and its memory with the square, so with a few
thousand observations it takes minutes for each model, and with many
thousands much longer.

For binary outcomes, with a single trial in each test observation, the
area, its limit and the band almost never point to miscalibration, even for
a wrong model and with any kind of test sets. They are not a check of
calibration there. A binomial model whose scored test observations all have
one trial, as with individual-level data that have several observations at
one location, is therefore treated apart. \code{assess_prediction()} says so, and
\code{\link[=plot_cross_validation]{plot_cross_validation()}}, \code{print()}, \code{summary()} and \code{\link[=to_table]{to_table()}} show its
curve and its area without the band or the limit, with a short note on why.
The result still has them. In an assessment of several models this applies
to each such model on its own. The CRPS of a binary outcome is the Brier
score \eqn{(p - y)^2} of the predictive probability \eqn{p} that
\eqn{y = 1}, and the scores still compare models.
}

\section{Failures}{
A failed refit or prediction does not stop the assessment. The scores of
that test set are missing and a warning reports the failures. So are those
of a test set of counts that can't be scored, because a draw of the mean is
not finite, is outside the range of the family or gives counts above
\eqn{2^{53}}, or because a binomial denominator is not a whole number.
}

